{:edb/db-before-t 1598 :edb/db-after-t 1599 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534911 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:45:24.090Z" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133559 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x$. Express $R$ as a type I region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\quad g_1(x) \\leq y \\leq g_2(x), \\quad f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}$." 13194139534911 false] [17592186133559 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x.$ Express $R$ as a type I region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: a \\leq x \\leq b,  \\quad g_1(x) \\leq y \\leq g_2(x), \\quad f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133559 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces $f_1(x,y)$ and $f_2(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=x^2+y^2$ and *above* by $z=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+y^2}_{f_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq  \\underbrace{2x}_{f_2(x,y)} .\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x - 1)^2 +  y^2 = 1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/2c16cae9e18867b3fa0d61203a6a91b9944e6595fa15bfd8d3b424346fefabee.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $y=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}$ and bounded *above* by $y=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_a \\leq x \\leq \\underbrace{2}_b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $R$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2,  \\:\\:-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2} \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}, \\:\\: x^2+y^2 \\leq z \\leq 2x\\right\\}\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133559 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/288733533249dbff97fbaa397a40cf7d.png)\n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces $f_1(x,y)$ and $f_2(x,y)$. Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand *above* by $z=2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+y^2}_{f_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq \\underbrace{2x}_{f_2(x,y)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z$. Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x + 1) + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle\n\n$$\n(x - 1)^2 + y^2 = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/8d32364434de168b5a340ae87c60abfe.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nand bounded *above* by\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_2(x)}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x$. From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_a \\leq x \\leq \\underbrace{2}_b\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $R$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\:-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2} \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}, \\:\\: x^2+y^2 \\leq z \\leq 2x\\right\\}\n$$" 13194139534911 false] [17592186133560 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y$. Express $R$ as a type II region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}$." 13194139534911 false] [17592186133560 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Express $R$ as a type II region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133560 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y - 1)^2 +  z^2 = 1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: (y - 1)^2 +  z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the front and back surfaces, we have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(y,z)=y^2+z^2$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(y,z)=2y.$\n\nTherefore, our type II representation is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:y^2+z^2 \\leq x \\leq 2y \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: (y - 1)^2 +  z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133560 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/088e0da77c5d0a30086a8df52ecb4549.png)\n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nx=y^2+z^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2y$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x$. Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y &= y^{2} + z^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y + z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(y^{2} - 2y + 1) + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} + z^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2 + z^2 = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: (y - 1)^2 + z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the front and back surfaces, we have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(y,z)=y^2+z^2$, and\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(y,z)=2y$.\n\nTherefore, our type II representation is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:y^2+z^2 \\leq x \\leq 2y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: (y - 1)^2 + z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139534911 false] [17592186133561 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1$. Express $R$ as a type III region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}$." 13194139534911 false] [17592186133561 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1.$ Express $R$ as a type III region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133561 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $x^2 +  2z^2 = 2$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: x^2 +  2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the left and right surfaces, we have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(x,z)=2x^2+z^2-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(x,z)=x^2-z^2+1.$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of our ellipse is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 2x^2+z^2-1 \\leq y \\leq x^2-z^2+1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: x^2 +  2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186133561 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/d50619957a793233d5ffe4220a746bf7.png)\n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid\n\n$$\ny=x^2-z^2+1\n$$\n\nand the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\ny=2x^2+z^2-1\n$$\n\nonto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y$. Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - z^{2} + 1 &= 2x^{2} + z^{2} - 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2z^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2z^2 = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: x^2 + 2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the left and right surfaces, we have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(x,z)=2x^2+z^2-1$, and\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(x,z)=x^2-z^2+1$.\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of our ellipse is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 2x^2+z^2-1 \\leq y \\leq x^2-z^2+1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: x^2 + 2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139534911 false] [17592186133741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319111 13194139534911 true] [17592186133741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319118 13194139534911 true] [17592186133741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319125 13194139534911 true] [17592186133741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319132 13194139534911 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319160 13194139534911 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319174 13194139534911 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319181 13194139534911 true] [17592186133742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319188 13194139534911 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319104 13194139534911 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319139 13194139534911 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319146 13194139534911 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319153 13194139534911 true] [17592186133743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319167 13194139534911 true] [17592186134047 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534911 true] [17592186134047 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and the plane $x = 2y$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): a \\le y \\le b, g_{1}(y) \\le z \\le g_{2}(y), f_{1}(y, z) \\le x \\le f_{2}(y, z)}$\nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(y, z) = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and $f_{2}(y, z) = 2y$\n2. $g_{1}(y) = 0$ and $g_{2}(y) = 1 - y^{2}$\n3. $a =-1$ and $b = 1$" 13194139534911 false] [17592186134047 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2y$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=0$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=1-{y}^{2}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186134047 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{2y}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186134424 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534911 true] [17592186134424 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/a266f4d3ab4254684c91cb422f96b70a4c9973f587b6afb2cd7af5db45e86561.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $z=4-x-y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186134424 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/q-73574.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x = 0$, $x = 1$, $y = 0$, $y = 2$, $z = 0$, and $z = 4 - x - y$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x, y) ∈ D, f_{1}(x, y) \\le z \\le f_{2}(x, y)}$\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2}$\n2. $f_{1}(x, y) = 1$ and $f_{2}(x, y) = 4 - x - y$\n3. $f_{1}(x, y) = 0$ and $f_{2}(x, y) = 2$" 13194139534911 false] [17592186134424 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319105 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1089 "q-160962" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=2$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1113 #uuid "8300dfff-606a-5dd2-9940-bcd5a083a1c8" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319104 1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319106 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319107 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319108 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319109 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319110 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1041 "selection" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319106 13194139534911 true] [17592186319105 1110 #uuid "9d678c9d-84d9-5a04-9028-9673f0ff916b" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319106 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319106 1111 #uuid "9589fbf8-6f34-5b1d-80c7-7dd54ad3f440" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319107 1034 "I only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319107 1111 #uuid "d20dd906-f60e-503c-b28a-8a1102a96bcb" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319108 1034 "III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319108 1111 #uuid "6aca794e-9976-5c07-8114-199e8dc9d522" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319109 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319109 1111 #uuid "45d86cd0-1cff-5eb4-841f-e7f64c2ef7f4" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319110 1034 "II only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319110 1111 #uuid "510b7d1e-071d-56ce-bb78-2361c38bf4ab" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319111 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319112 13194139534911 true] [17592186319111 1089 "q-161117" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319111 1091 #edb/ref 17592