1145 "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319141 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319142 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319143 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319144 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319145 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1041 "selection" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319141 13194139534911 true] [17592186319140 1110 #uuid "235f23da-b400-5f67-a27d-1dffddd03b46" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319141 1034 "II and III only" 131941395