orkers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319148 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319149 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319150 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319151 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319152 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1041 "selection" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319149 13194139534911 true] [17592186319147 1110 #uuid "26917a48-e0c0-5cae-bb06-5a113fc235ed" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319148 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319148 1111 #uuid "49dff8e7-6a13-525a-8efe-dc05441988e7" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319149 1034 "I and III on