gion.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319155 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319156 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319157 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319158 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319159 13194139534911 true] [17592186319154 1040 #edb/re