e] [17592186319172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319172 1111 #uuid "dcc75e0f-049d-503d-b129-2d687420dbaf" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319173 1034 "II only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319173 1111 #uuid "413d7195-58fd-5ebd-8512-065097d0d33a" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319175 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1089 "q-161380" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1113 #uuid "48692a91-498d-57fa-a26f-e5919ca33a68" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319174 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319176 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319177 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319178 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319179 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319180 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1041 "selection" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319176 13194139534911 true] [17592186319175 1110 #uuid "b18f45e1-6639-5fc8-a324-c3b915e4e11b" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319176 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319176 1111 #uuid "a73582a9-fba1-5440-804e-94e0eb75a902" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319177 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319177 1111 #uuid "335238e8-679c-50e9-b905-cb96b764848e" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319178 1034 "I only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319178 1111 #uuid "55aac357-0760-503c-b2d1-e2df49a0b0ce" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319179 1034 "III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319179 1111 #uuid "b93851fa-3375-55c8-b006-38c5fcec3737" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319180 1034 "II only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319180 1111 #uuid "cc3a2685-58c9-5367-a915-d33c28b2ddd0" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319182 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1089 "q-162530" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1100 "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1113 #uuid "2dde6173-a3cc-5755-a44a-46e205adb04c" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319181 1145 "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319183 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319184 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319185 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319186 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319187 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1041 "selection" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186319187 13194139534911 true] [17592186319182 1110 #uuid "ac38b176-d0cd-5323-94cb-c2f6e7b66131" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319183 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534911 true] [17592186319183 1111 #uuid "dd3beaa1-47b5-5908-afa1-3cbe51db36fb" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319184 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534911 true] [17592186319184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13